  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Ortho, para directing groups are electron-donating groups; meta directing groups are electron-withdrawing groups. The halide ions, which are electron-withdrawing but ortho, para directing, are the exception.</p><p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>OH</mi></math> group is strong electron donating group. Hence electrophile can easily give electron.</p><p><math><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> group in phenol can release electrons to the ring better than <math><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math> group in toluene. <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></math> atom has electron withdrawing effect which inhibits electrophile attack readily.</p><p><br/><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0035_55b64f80600c.png\" style=\"width: 566px; height: 112px;\"/></p>"
  }
